ems  like sort of a slow down is the very  informal way that i would describe it as  a guy who's now 78 years old rather than  70 when he was debating paul ryan  but from your perspective which is a  trained eye  can you talk a little bit about what's  different about those details and what  you see with joe biden versus donald  trump  well first of all i uh would  refrain from making any specific  comments because  i have not observed mr biden enough  um also i  don't generally comment on public  figures unless they pose  a danger to society and a danger to the  public's well-being  which is my threshold for commenting on  public figures  and so what i will say is that there  have been  uh not really glaring signs that would  make me concerned about any catastrophic  danger to the public  i uh i do notice the things that other  people have remarked about the changes  over time  and also the the different  gaffes that many people have remarked on  which for the most part i will ha uh i  will in general just say  that they are uh that they would  uh fall under a more superficial  category  than the ones that we see with donald  trump which are  truly serious impairments that would  concern us  about catastrophic danger to  the nation's well-being if not the world  how can we tell the difference and and  maybe i mean  i'm curious what would make it rise or  not  rise to that threshold of danger that  you talk about  so um i told you about the  violence-proneness and  uh the the the apparent mental  incapacity that  mr trump has exhibited um  mainly what we'd be concerned about in  terms of public safety  are dangerousness and unfitness  and uh with regard to dangerousness it  would be  um the president's uh  apparent violent violence proneness that  that he has already shown  and that he has actually demonstrated  throughout this presidency and actually  placed our nation in danger multiple  times  um uh i mean there there  could be a whole list he has brought us  to the brink of war  numerous times he has taunted  other powers he has severed um  or weakened our uh our relationships  with allies  he has destabilized  our position geopolitically  these are the kinds of things that would  be particularly dangerous  and in terms of unfitness we actually  did do  a mental capacity evaluation on the  president when the correct information  became available  now this is uh the special counsel  robert mueller's report which contained  multiple reports from close associates  and  and co-workers who had direct  interactions with the president  and if the report was not uh enough to  indict a president it was certainly  enough  to assess mental capacity that is  the capacity to fulfill the duties of  his office  now without the basic medical uh the  mental capacity  there's no chance that he could be  mentally fit that he could be fit for  duty  uh and he in fact failed every criterion  uh the criterion include the the ability  to take in  information and facts uh advice from his  advisors is necessary to the situation  the ability to uh to be flexible and  manipulate the information he receives  not be bound to uh fixed ideas or  conspiracy theories  to be able to make decisions that are  sound  reality based and rational  that consider consequences and are not  done impulsively and that he goes back  and forth after making this  decision these are all  factors we consider in a mental capacity  evaluation so that  he failed definitively and for that we  do not need a personal interview  although we did invite him for an  interview in case  he considered himself fit and would like  to give us information that might  overturn our findings but this was uh  definitive and it was done by  independent top mental health experts uh  a panel of them  and it was done for uh in service of the  public  in order to inform the public of what it  was facing  um now these are the kinds of  information we look for  when when uh we suspect a person would  be dangerous  in the president's position and these  are not the kinds of information we  found  in mr biden but we have certainly  recommended that any  uh candidate uh for the presidency or  vice presidency  submit themselves to an independent  expert panel who can do a fitness for  duty exam  uh it is fairly um it is fairly rapid  it's not a diagnostic exam it doesn't  involve  a review of one's entire medical history  or medical records  it simply assesses whether or not the  person is ready for duty  and uh so we definitely do recommend  this of mr biden himself  so that was going to be the last thing i  was going to ask and you've sort of you  sort of addressed it which is  certainly if we were to require  or recommend or request that  anybody who might be president be  screened  it seems very clear that the montreal  cognitive assessment  falls dramatically short of counting as  as anything definitive as you've pointed  out  uh and and would would the furthest that  you could go  be a full neuropsychiatric evaluation  with a full review of medical files like  would that be the complete opposite end  and you're recommending something that  is  that is not quite so so involved  uh yes um it would allow for  a diagnosis which would also be able to  give a prognosis  in terms of his trajectory over time and  and also enable us to treat him for any  conditions that  would be of uh imminent danger to his  own personal health which would then by  extension  affect his fitness for duty so yes that  is something that we would  recommend but above all he needs  an examination by an independent panel  not tied by his uh by  under uh employment by the white house  or uh not politically um  involved in terms of their potential  promotion and so on  and uh this is also where uh the  american psychiatric association  needs to rescind its uh  political compromise which has gone only  in one direction  only in the direction of not commenting  on a public figure  regardless of need when there are  specifically  areas where we ought to comment in order  to  improve the public health and to do our  professional  responsibility to society which is  absolutely  one of our duties as psychiatrists and  mental health professionals  in addition to seeing patients and  treating  uh individuals we've been speaking with  dr  bandy x lee who is a yale university  forensic psychiatrist  i so appreciate your time and and thank  you so much for sharing your thoughts  with our audience  thank you so much for having me
 

